1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,858
A client asked our agent
for a one specific flight.

2
00:00:03,077 --> 00:00:07,261
The agent bought a ticket on a different
one, and every check in our code said yes.

3
00:00:07,877 --> 00:00:11,206
The flight they asked for wasn't
in the search result, so the system

4
00:00:11,211 --> 00:00:14,402
took the closest match on its own,
and every check looked only at

5
00:00:14,418 --> 00:00:15,798
the flight the system had picked.

6
00:00:16,283 --> 00:00:20,051
An operator caught it, but only after
the ticket was already paid for.

7
00:00:20,721 --> 00:00:25,039
I'm Danila from the core AI team of a
travel tech company, our agent issuing

8
00:00:25,041 --> 00:00:28,456
an airline tickets and managing the
business trips for our corporate clients.

9
00:00:29,535 --> 00:00:33,707
To show how it happens, I rebuilt it
in a test store and ran over a thousand

10
00:00:33,709 --> 00:00:35,879
support chats with today's cloud models.

11
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:39,989
Dozen of times, the code check
approved a refund for the wrong order.

12
00:00:41,036 --> 00:00:44,909
Some conditions only a model can judge,
and when a check trusts the model's

13
00:00:44,947 --> 00:00:47,216
answer, the evidence is self-signed.

14
00:00:48,012 --> 00:00:51,161
You'll leave today with the four
questions for any action of your

15
00:00:51,161 --> 00:00:53,236
agent that can cost money or trust.

16
00:00:54,396 --> 00:00:55,569
Here is some of those chats.

17
00:00:57,057 --> 00:01:00,130
The story is invented, and
everything it does is ordinary.

18
00:01:00,412 --> 00:01:03,066
A customer writes, "My
blender arrived broken.

19
00:01:03,140 --> 00:01:04,134
I want my money back."

20
00:01:04,968 --> 00:01:06,514
The support agent is an LLM.

21
00:01:06,696 --> 00:01:08,740
It reads the chat and calls two tools.

22
00:01:08,985 --> 00:01:10,765
One finds the customer's order.

23
00:01:10,890 --> 00:01:13,156
The second sends a refund.

24
00:01:13,936 --> 00:01:17,829
Before any money moves, plain code runs
a final check against the store policy.

25
00:01:18,348 --> 00:01:24,265
Refund within thirty days of delivery and
up to two hundred dollars without a human.

26
00:01:25,625 --> 00:01:27,195
The agent find two orders.

27
00:01:27,462 --> 00:01:32,030
The customer bought the same blender
twice, a hundred, and eighty dollars each.

28
00:01:32,518 --> 00:01:36,624
The chat doesn't say which one,
so agent proposes the order

29
00:01:36,656 --> 00:01:38,615
eleven eighty-seven and asks.

30
00:01:39,756 --> 00:01:40,721
Customer said yes.

31
00:01:41,268 --> 00:01:42,526
The final check runs.

32
00:01:42,565 --> 00:01:43,864
Is it a customer's order?

33
00:01:44,005 --> 00:01:45,396
Yes, says database.

34
00:01:45,444 --> 00:01:46,982
Delivered in the last thirty days?

35
00:01:47,239 --> 00:01:49,264
Yes, nine days ago, says the carrier.

36
00:01:49,786 --> 00:01:51,910
And under the two hundred dollars?

37
00:01:52,002 --> 00:01:53,454
Yes, says the order record.

38
00:01:54,166 --> 00:01:57,590
Every check is green, and a hundred
and eighty dollars just go out

39
00:01:59,515 --> 00:02:00,731
But which blender was broken?

40
00:02:01,487 --> 00:02:05,825
The first one, order 1042,
delivered 70 days ago.

41
00:02:06,062 --> 00:02:09,152
The customer bought the second
blender as a replacement.

42
00:02:09,558 --> 00:02:12,907
The first one is too old to refund,
and the money went to the replacement.

43
00:02:13,647 --> 00:02:16,286
Nobody attacked the store
and the customer didn't lie.

44
00:02:16,389 --> 00:02:17,937
This is non-adversarial failure.

45
00:02:18,345 --> 00:02:21,152
The check did exactly
what it was written to do.

46
00:02:22,050 --> 00:02:24,693
Actually, the fix is small
and I'll show it, but nothing

47
00:02:24,693 --> 00:02:26,378
here tells that you need it.

48
00:02:27,764 --> 00:02:32,126
The question for this talk, all
your agent checks are green.

49
00:02:32,301 --> 00:02:34,889
Will they still be green
if the agent got it wrong?

50
00:02:36,537 --> 00:02:39,013
Now, the four question one at a time.

51
00:02:39,168 --> 00:02:40,712
The first is, who decides?

52
00:02:42,174 --> 00:02:46,983
The first two question are the familiar
ones, and our store gets both right.

53
00:02:47,530 --> 00:02:48,301
It still pays.

54
00:02:48,912 --> 00:02:51,051
Prompt instructions are
the soft constraints.

55
00:02:51,841 --> 00:02:54,483
So in our store, the refund
decision is made in code.

56
00:02:55,299 --> 00:02:57,772
The model extracts the
order and the reason.

57
00:02:58,020 --> 00:03:01,888
A policy check reads the
order records and decides.

58
00:03:02,558 --> 00:03:05,426
Even the reply, "Refund
approved," is written by code.

59
00:03:05,782 --> 00:03:07,445
But the model still picks the order.

60
00:03:07,801 --> 00:03:08,559
Keep it in mind.

61
00:03:09,347 --> 00:03:13,239
The second question is, who writes
the words the customer reads?

62
00:03:15,204 --> 00:03:16,822
Support bots make up policies.

63
00:03:17,054 --> 00:03:22,278
In 2022, Air Canada's chatbot
promised a passenger a refund rule

64
00:03:23,032 --> 00:03:26,395
the airline didn't have, and the
tribunal made the airline pay.

65
00:03:26,995 --> 00:03:30,728
So in our store, policy texts
come from the system of records.

66
00:03:30,791 --> 00:03:35,664
The model selects the clause ID and
code renders that clause verbatim.

67
00:03:36,324 --> 00:03:38,426
A wrong clause only quotes a real policy.

68
00:03:39,188 --> 00:03:42,894
A wrong code sends real money
back to the checks that paid.

69
00:03:43,846 --> 00:03:46,106
Everything that decides is code.

70
00:03:46,196 --> 00:03:47,436
So why did it pay?

71
00:03:48,226 --> 00:03:50,987
Look at the provenance of
every value the check reads.

72
00:03:51,457 --> 00:03:54,847
The owner came from the database,
the delivery date from the carrier,

73
00:03:54,909 --> 00:03:56,970
the amounts from the order records.

74
00:03:57,443 --> 00:04:01,585
Every value had a good source
except one, the order ID.

75
00:04:01,711 --> 00:04:02,446
The model chose it.

76
00:04:03,405 --> 00:04:05,866
The carrier gave the right
date for the wrong order.

77
00:04:07,107 --> 00:04:09,475
Code can't check this on itself.

78
00:04:09,987 --> 00:04:14,539
Matching my blender to an order
is entity resolution, and from

79
00:04:14,539 --> 00:04:16,119
a chat, only a model can do it.

80
00:04:16,885 --> 00:04:19,409
The same goes for flags
like the customer agreed.

81
00:04:19,885 --> 00:04:23,403
The model hands the code, the
result, an ID, a flag, a score,

82
00:04:23,896 --> 00:04:25,307
and the guardrails trusted.

83
00:04:26,258 --> 00:04:27,670
There are public cases too.

84
00:04:27,897 --> 00:04:33,349
In February, someone asked an AI agent on
Solana for about three hundred dollars.

85
00:04:33,781 --> 00:04:38,423
It bought tokens for about that
much, then mistook its whole balance

86
00:04:38,435 --> 00:04:40,258
for that purchase and sent it all.

87
00:04:40,795 --> 00:04:45,140
Tokens was roughly four hundred and fifty
thousand dollars at the market price.

88
00:04:45,843 --> 00:04:46,752
Nobody hacked it.

89
00:04:47,025 --> 00:04:50,189
The transfer was valid, right
signature, enough balance.

90
00:04:50,895 --> 00:04:53,113
The amount just came from the agent.

91
00:04:53,277 --> 00:04:55,269
And how often does it happens?

92
00:04:56,158 --> 00:05:00,174
So I counted a public
benchmark, τ²-bench.

93
00:05:00,691 --> 00:05:04,209
it's a public test of support
agents, an invented store, a

94
00:05:04,210 --> 00:05:08,647
simulated customer, and tool that
cancel, change, or return orders.

95
00:05:09,769 --> 00:05:12,279
Each task say which order should change.

96
00:05:12,977 --> 00:05:16,111
I took sixteen hundred
published conversation of four

97
00:05:16,151 --> 00:05:17,429
twenty twenty-five models.

98
00:05:18,013 --> 00:05:22,711
In eighty-two, the store tools accepted
a change to an order two task never

99
00:05:22,714 --> 00:05:28,205
touched In at least twenty-one,
the model picks that order itself.

100
00:05:29,068 --> 00:05:30,275
Now, my models do it less.

101
00:05:30,434 --> 00:05:34,198
Seventeen in about four thousand,
and in sixteen of those, the customer

102
00:05:34,233 --> 00:05:35,992
has said yes to the agent summary.

103
00:05:36,573 --> 00:05:42,655
The customers there are simulated, so
this shows that it happens, not how often.

104
00:05:43,316 --> 00:05:46,482
So I rebuild the blender store
with today's Claude models.

105
00:05:46,767 --> 00:05:50,489
Here's what Claude Sonnet wrote in my
store with both blenders in the list.

106
00:05:51,010 --> 00:05:54,585
I assume you mean eleven
eighty-seven since it within

107
00:05:54,632 --> 00:05:56,262
our thirty days refund window.

108
00:05:56,772 --> 00:05:58,341
The customer said, "Yes, please."

109
00:05:58,542 --> 00:06:01,200
A hundred and eighty dollars
went to the wrong blender.

110
00:06:02,831 --> 00:06:05,555
The model picked that order
because it would pass the checks.

111
00:06:05,898 --> 00:06:07,784
I call this self-signed evidence.

112
00:06:08,634 --> 00:06:12,139
A field, the check trusts
written by the system it checks.

113
00:06:12,352 --> 00:06:16,268
Your browser rejects a self-sign
certificate even when the math is fine

114
00:06:16,831 --> 00:06:18,752
because the site vouches for itself.

115
00:06:19,191 --> 00:06:23,062
In banking terms, the agent is
both the maker and the checker.

116
00:06:23,950 --> 00:06:27,487
A check proves only what
the agent could not shape.

117
00:06:28,017 --> 00:06:29,895
So why not to put a second model on it?

118
00:06:30,678 --> 00:06:34,787
I took seventy-two wrong refunds
from those runs and seventy-two

119
00:06:34,789 --> 00:06:39,344
right ones and had Claude Sonnet
review each one as an LLM judge.

120
00:06:40,073 --> 00:06:43,340
Ask to review, it
pre-approves every wrong one.

121
00:06:44,053 --> 00:06:48,009
Then I ask directly, "Is this
the order the customer meant?"

122
00:06:48,480 --> 00:06:51,649
Reading the agent's vote, it
still approves all seventy-two.

123
00:06:52,346 --> 00:06:56,472
Reading the whole chats and every
order, it caught fifty and blocked

124
00:06:56,483 --> 00:06:57,970
forty-one right refunds too.

125
00:06:59,166 --> 00:07:04,235
I can see that the chat is unclear,
but not which order the customer meant.

126
00:07:05,039 --> 00:07:09,143
Where the store really lost money,
it's approved twenty out of twenty.

127
00:07:10,241 --> 00:07:14,036
The customer confirmed
order eleven eighty-seven.

128
00:07:14,442 --> 00:07:17,880
That yes answers the agent's own proposal.

129
00:07:18,345 --> 00:07:22,244
So even the chat is partly
written by the system it checks.

130
00:07:23,270 --> 00:07:27,441
One judge, my own scenarios
take it as a direction.

131
00:07:28,447 --> 00:07:31,303
The third question, who wrote each field?

132
00:07:31,871 --> 00:07:33,106
Start with the order ID.

133
00:07:33,785 --> 00:07:38,403
The model can propose an order, but it
has to point to what the customer wrote.

134
00:07:38,896 --> 00:07:43,340
Code checks that those votes are in
the chat and fits only one order.

135
00:07:43,769 --> 00:07:46,908
Then the refunds go through
without another question.

136
00:07:47,721 --> 00:07:52,303
Here, my blender fits two
orders, so code asks with

137
00:07:52,346 --> 00:07:54,130
options straight from the record.

138
00:07:55,215 --> 00:08:00,170
Our customer picks the August
blender, seventy days, so no refunds.

139
00:08:00,641 --> 00:08:05,744
After a yes, thirty-five, thirty-five
refunds out of a hundred and eighty

140
00:08:05,848 --> 00:08:12,120
went to the wrong order When the
choice came from the records, none did.

141
00:08:12,978 --> 00:08:15,680
Every scenario here was
ambiguous on purpose.

142
00:08:15,826 --> 00:08:18,616
So every customer got that question.

143
00:08:18,772 --> 00:08:20,873
It costs one extra message.

144
00:08:21,484 --> 00:08:23,599
But a new check can be wrong too.

145
00:08:24,288 --> 00:08:25,310
That's the fourth question.

146
00:08:26,130 --> 00:08:26,951
Who checked the check?

147
00:08:27,373 --> 00:08:28,402
Break it on purpose.

148
00:08:29,121 --> 00:08:31,933
Step one, the test I
promised is a negative test.

149
00:08:32,166 --> 00:08:36,330
Take a locked refunds, swap each order
for another valid order of the same

150
00:08:36,332 --> 00:08:38,024
customer, and run the check again.

151
00:08:38,741 --> 00:08:41,343
The plan check stayed green on every swap.

152
00:08:41,398 --> 00:08:44,556
So did a customer confirmed
flag kept in the session.

153
00:08:45,133 --> 00:08:47,810
Only the check that reads the
customer choice turned red.

154
00:08:48,893 --> 00:08:50,495
Step two is fault injection.

155
00:08:50,731 --> 00:08:51,987
Make the agent wrong.

156
00:08:52,531 --> 00:08:53,952
I told it not to ask.

157
00:08:54,367 --> 00:09:00,146
Every customer who mends the order blender
got the wrong refunds, 30 out of 30.

158
00:09:00,405 --> 00:09:02,177
When code asks, none did.

159
00:09:03,136 --> 00:09:07,302
Step three, run the new check next
to the old one in shadow mode.

160
00:09:07,857 --> 00:09:12,708
It sees every real refunds and locks
what it would block, but blocks nothing

161
00:09:12,709 --> 00:09:18,712
yet A checks that refuses an honest
customer costs money and trust too.

162
00:09:19,712 --> 00:09:24,513
A support lead labels, labels a
sample with ground truth from the

163
00:09:24,550 --> 00:09:28,135
order records, not from the chats,
and you read every disagreement.

164
00:09:28,899 --> 00:09:31,389
Only then does the check get to block.

165
00:09:31,848 --> 00:09:35,793
If the same model labels your
ref- reference sets, you're back

166
00:09:35,795 --> 00:09:37,337
to the self-signed evidence.

167
00:09:38,176 --> 00:09:43,432
Take one action that costs money or
trust when it goes wrong: a refund,

168
00:09:43,760 --> 00:09:45,765
a payment, a ticket purchase.

169
00:09:46,197 --> 00:09:47,532
Ask four questions about it.

170
00:09:48,070 --> 00:09:48,885
Who decides?

171
00:09:49,143 --> 00:09:51,046
Obviously, code, not a model.

172
00:09:51,712 --> 00:09:52,844
Who writes the words?

173
00:09:53,209 --> 00:09:54,341
The system of records.

174
00:09:55,017 --> 00:09:56,163
Who wrote each field?

175
00:09:57,058 --> 00:10:00,076
The customer's words or
records checked by code.

176
00:10:01,028 --> 00:10:02,103
Who checks the check?

177
00:10:02,507 --> 00:10:04,826
Break it on purpose,
then run it a schedule.

178
00:10:05,608 --> 00:10:06,908
None of this is actually new.

179
00:10:07,062 --> 00:10:10,897
In nineteen eighty-eight, Norm
Hardy called it the confused deputy,

180
00:10:11,366 --> 00:10:14,630
a program with authority acting
on a name someone else gave it.

181
00:10:15,282 --> 00:10:19,034
Our refund tool has the authority,
and the model gives it the name.

182
00:10:19,907 --> 00:10:22,154
Back to the tickets from
the start of this talk.

183
00:10:22,515 --> 00:10:26,335
Every check in our code said yes,
and every check looked at the rough

184
00:10:26,336 --> 00:10:28,014
flights the system had picked.

185
00:10:28,479 --> 00:10:29,855
None of them asked who picked it.

186
00:10:30,606 --> 00:10:31,719
You still need a model.

187
00:10:31,832 --> 00:10:34,081
Only a model can read
what a customer means.

188
00:10:34,130 --> 00:10:39,284
So let it propose and make your
check ask where that model came from.

189
00:10:40,287 --> 00:10:43,236
Open your final check and write
down every value it reads.

190
00:10:43,303 --> 00:10:48,577
Next to each one, write who wrote it:
the database, the customer, or the model.

191
00:10:49,318 --> 00:10:52,683
Every value with the model next
to is a self-signed evidence.

192
00:10:53,204 --> 00:10:56,729
Then take a look, replace
that value with another valid

193
00:10:56,729 --> 00:10:59,207
one, and run the check again.

194
00:10:59,754 --> 00:11:04,306
If it stays green, it can't tell
the right value from the wrong one.

195
00:11:04,571 --> 00:11:07,841
Better to learn that from your
logs than from your customer.

196
00:11:08,681 --> 00:11:11,576
Would your check still be green
if the agent got it wrong?

197
00:11:12,116 --> 00:11:13,630
A green check is not a fact.

198
00:11:13,731 --> 00:11:14,563
It's a question.

199
00:11:14,605 --> 00:11:15,699
Who signed it?

200
00:11:17,417 --> 00:11:18,332
I'm on LinkedIn.

201
00:11:18,478 --> 00:11:19,594
The link is on the screen.

202
00:11:19,904 --> 00:11:23,840
If you run this test on your own
agent, tell me what you find.

203
00:11:24,416 --> 00:11:24,899
Thank you.
